The note says: "the search indexer needs the product catalog (product documents)."

This is a dependency relation: indexer depends_on product catalog.

Existing entities: "indexer" and "product catalog". The note says "search indexer" — that likely refers to the "indexer" entity. Should I add an alias? The note uses "search indexer" while existing entity is "indexer". It's plausibly the same thing. Per instructions: "When the note uses a different name for an existing entity, add an entity_aliases entry mapping the note's name to the existing name." So add alias "search indexer" → "indexer". Evidence: "the search indexer needs the product catalog".

Add relation: src "indexer", dst "product catalog", rel_kind "depends_on", evidence "the search indexer needs the product catalog".

Also could add a fact? The relation captures the dependency. Maybe also add a fact: "The indexer depends on the product catalog for product documents." Hmm. The instructions: relations for relationships; facts for new durable facts. A dependency is a relation. Adding both a fact and relation could be duplicative but the schema allows facts about new durable facts. I think adding the relation is the primary. But adding a standalone fact stating the dependency might also be reasonable. The memory patch systems typically want relations for the graph; facts for statements. To be safe, I'll add both? Actually, the instruction says "A fact is one durable statement the note makes". The dependency is durable. But relations capture it. I think adding just the relation plus alias is cleanest. However, adding a fact doesn't hurt. Hmm — the note is purely about dependency; the relation schema handles it. I'll add the relation and the alias, and optionally a fact. I'll include the fact too since it's a durable statement: "The indexer depends on the product catalog for product documents." Entities: indexer, product catalog. Evidence: "the search indexer needs the product catalog (product documents)".

Actually, to avoid redundancy, maybe just relation. But typical expected output: add_facts may include the dependency as a fact too. The instructions treat facts and relations as complementary. I'll include both — a fact and a relation.

